; 32-bindop.kurt

; `bindop` marks a symbol as a *variable-binding operator*: its first
; argument is the variable (or a condition on it, e.g. `forall x>0 F(x)`) that
; is then considered bound inside the rest of the expression.  `forall` and `exists` (used already in lessons
; 19-let.kurt and 20-pick.kurt) are declared exactly this way -- as of this
; writing, directly as part of Kurt's hard-coded core, not by `logic.kurt`
; (only their axioms, "forall-elim" etc., still come from there):
;
;     arity  forall 2
;     bindop forall
;
; A symbol needs an `arity` (31-arity.kurt) before it can be declared a
; `bindop`. The binding scope is fixed semantics, so `bindop` also makes the
; symbol a constant and prints that implied declaration. Here is a minimal binder of our own:

arity myforall 2
bindop myforall

bool P
const P
arity P 1

parse myforall x (P x)   ; (myforall x (P x)) -- `x` is bound in `(P x)`

; With a condition, the binder binds the first symbol of the condition, as
; written, that is new or a variable -- constants, predicates and the variables
; of enclosing binders are skipped. Kurt decides it when it reads the line and
; keeps it: the binder binds the same variable for the rest of the file (also
; when a block closes and its constants are free again; doc/kurt-doc.md §4.4).

const c
load numbers
use myforall (c < y) (P y)          ; binds `y`: `c` is a constant
use myforall (P z) (z = z)          ; binds `z`: `P` is a predicate
use myforall (0 < e) (myforall (e < w) (0 < w))   ; the inner one binds `w`: `e` is bound outside
expect "TypeError" "no variable to bind"
    use myforall (c < 1) (P c)      ; every symbol is a constant

; `bindop` sets up scope and checks that the first argument really is an
; unbound variable; the actual
; reasoning rules ("forall-elim" etc.) are ordinary `use` axioms, exactly
; like the ones in `logic.kurt`.
